package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"strings"

	"github.com/geoffmilleraz/app-kit/appkit/ui/tokens"
)

// This file exists because of where an agent is standing when it gets theming
// wrong. get_kit_theming is the surface an agent reaches at the exact moment it
// is choosing colors for markup it just pulled — which is the moment the
// hazards below become reachable. Every one of them shipped to production and
// survived code review, so "read the rule carefully" is a demonstrated
// non-solution; each entry pairs the rule with the measurement or command that
// catches it.
//
// The full reviewer checklist (procedures, the DevTools audit, the kit's open
// findings) lives in docs/theming-hazards.md; this is the condensed form that
// rides along with the theming guide. The floors come from tokens so the two
// cannot disagree about the number.

// themingHazards renders the hazard checklist appended to the theming guide.
func themingHazards() string {
	var b strings.Builder

	b.WriteString("\n## Hazards — the ones that shipped\n\n")
	b.WriteString("These are not style opinions. Each one reached production and passed review, ")
	b.WriteString("because none of them looks wrong in source. Full checklist: `docs/theming-hazards.md`.\n\n")

	b.WriteString("### 1. Never key a color on a theme selector\n\n")
	b.WriteString("A rule scoped to `[data-theme=\"light\"]` also fires under every variant that ")
	b.WriteString("remaps the palette without touching that attribute. In the kit a **theme is a ")
	b.WriteString("whole token set** swapped at the `@import` — nothing in the DOM changes — and ")
	b.WriteString("the shipped `synthwave` set is dark-valued in both schemes, so `data-theme=\"light\"` ")
	b.WriteString("selects a *dark* palette under it. Measured cost when this shipped elsewhere: ")
	b.WriteString("ground at 8.51:1 against figure at 3.39:1 — inverted.\n\n")
	b.WriteString("- **Wrong:** `[data-theme=\"light\"] .mark { fill: #333 }`\n")
	b.WriteString("- **Right:** `fill=\"var(--ak-color-ink)\"` / `class=\"text-ink\"` — the role carries both scheme values.\n")
	b.WriteString("- **If a rule truly must differ by scheme:** use `dark:` (generated by the token set itself). There is no `light:` variant on purpose.\n")
	b.WriteString("- The correct number of scheme-gated color rules in kit code is **zero**: `grep -rn 'data-theme=\"light\"' appkit apps`.\n\n")

	b.WriteString("### 2. Equal specificity — and legends that restate a color\n\n")
	b.WriteString("`.mark.is-used {opacity:1}` and `[data-theme=\"light\"] .mark {opacity:.24}` are ")
	b.WriteString("both (0,3,0); the later one wins, and the later one is usually the generic one. ")
	b.WriteString("The load-bearing mark shipped at **1.30:1**. Scope the generic rule with ")
	b.WriteString("`:not(.is-used)` so the states are disjoint rather than racing.\n\n")
	b.WriteString("Structurally: **a legend must reuse the mark's classes, or be produced by the ")
	b.WriteString("same function as the mark**, so a key that disagrees with the field is ")
	b.WriteString("unrepresentable. Prior art in the kit: `viz.GradedRole(frac)` is exported ")
	b.WriteString("precisely so related elements color consistently. Note that SVG presentation ")
	b.WriteString("attributes (`fill=\"var(--ak-color-…)\"`, what `ui/viz` emits) have specificity ")
	b.WriteString("**0** — any app stylesheet rule beats them.\n\n")

	b.WriteString("### 3. Measure contrast, never read it\n\n")
	b.WriteString("Two review rounds missed marks at **1.30:1** and **1.66:1**; in one case a ")
	b.WriteString("builder reported validating a light-theme color ramp **it had never written**. ")
	b.WriteString("A contrast claim that does not name an instrument, a selector, a theme and a ")
	b.WriteString("number is not a claim.\n\n")
	b.WriteString("```go\n")
	b.WriteString("tokens.Contrast(fg, bg)                        // WCAG ratio for two token values\n")
	b.WriteString("tokens.ContrastOver(fg, alpha, bg)             // composited — the opacity case\n")
	b.WriteString("tokens.ContrastRoles(tok, \"caution\", \"surface\", \"light\")\n")
	b.WriteString("```\n\n")
	b.WriteString("It errors on anything it cannot resolve (`var()`, `color-mix()`, a translucent ")
	b.WriteString("value with no background) rather than returning a number that reads like a pass. ")
	b.WriteString("Print the kit's whole table with `go test ./appkit/ui/tokens -run ")
	b.WriteString("TestKitPaletteMeaningFloor -v`. Composited styles (ancestor opacity, `color-mix`, ")
	b.WriteString("what is actually behind the mark) need a browser — the DevTools audit script is ")
	b.WriteString("in `docs/theming-hazards.md`, and must be run for every theme **and** every ")
	b.WriteString("token set that ships.\n\n")

	b.WriteString("### 4. A token that does not exist fails silently\n\n")
	b.WriteString("`var(--ak-color-typo)` without a fallback is invalid at computed-value time: the ")
	b.WriteString("declaration is dropped and the property falls back to its initial value (black ")
	b.WriteString("fill, no border). A utility for a role the active token set lacks is never ")
	b.WriteString("emitted at all — the class is inert, with nothing in the built CSS to grep for. ")
	b.WriteString("Neither produces an error.\n\n")
	b.WriteString("Audited 2026-08-07: the kit's built stylesheet has **no dangling references** — ")
	b.WriteString("every undefined name it references carries an explicit fallback (`--ak-font-scale`, ")
	b.WriteString("Tailwind's own `--tw-*`/`--default-font-*`), which is correct usage. One live ")
	b.WriteString("instance of the second mode does exist and is pinned by a test: ")
	b.WriteString("`appkit/ui/kanban` styles a hover with `text-neon-pink`, a role only the ")
	b.WriteString("synthwave theme defines. Rule: a component in `appkit/ui/` references only roles ")
	b.WriteString("the **default** set defines; a component that accepts a caller-supplied role emits ")
	b.WriteString("a fallback. Detect with `go test ./appkit/ui/tokens -run ")
	b.WriteString("'TestNoDanglingRoleReferences|TestNoThemeOnlyRoleUtilities'`.\n\n")

	fmt.Fprintf(&b, "### 5. The %.0f:1 floor, and opacity encodings\n\n", tokens.MeaningFloor)
	b.WriteString("Anything that carries meaning — chart mark, status dot, graded fill, focus ring, ")
	fmt.Fprintf(&b, "legend swatch — clears **%.0f:1** against the background it is actually drawn on "+
		"(`tokens.MeaningFloor`); text clears **%.1f:1** (`tokens.TextFloor`).\n\n",
		tokens.MeaningFloor, tokens.TextFloor)
	b.WriteString("Opacity-based intensity is the usual way this fails, because opacity is a move ")
	b.WriteString("toward the background. Measured on the kit's own palette (light): `accent` on ")
	b.WriteString("`surface` is 5.97:1 at full strength, 3.29:1 at 0.70, 2.25:1 at 0.50, and 1.19:1 ")
	b.WriteString("at 0.12. The kit's `viz.Heatmap` ramp (`0.12 + 0.85·norm`) therefore sits below ")
	b.WriteString("the floor for roughly its bottom two-thirds. One role fails the floor even at ")
	b.WriteString("full strength: **`caution` on `surface` measures 2.60:1 in light** (fine in dark ")
	b.WriteString("at 9.62:1), so a light-scheme mark colored `caution` needs a label or a border ")
	b.WriteString("carrying the same meaning. Fixes, in order of preference: ")
	b.WriteString("decide whether the mark carries meaning at all (a bar's track does not); floor ")
	b.WriteString("the ramp where it measures the floor; add a second channel (label, size, ")
	b.WriteString("position) so opacity is redundancy; or use distinct roles plus a legend built ")
	b.WriteString("from the same expression as the marks.\n")

	return b.String()
}
